<% seo title: "Privacy policy · Tastatur",
       description: "How this instance handles its account holders' own data: what is held, how " \
                    "long it is kept, who else sees it, and the rights you have over it.",
       og_type: "article" %>
<% entity = Tastatur.legal_value(:entity) %>
<% contact = Tastatur.legal_value(:email) %>

<div class="shell max-w-3xl">
  <div class="pt-5 rule-heavy mb-8">
    <p class="label mt-5 mb-3">Legal</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em]">Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="text-ink-2 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      How <%= entity %> handles data about <strong class="text-ink">you</strong>, the person
      holding a Tastatur account.
    </p>
  </div>

  <%= render "compliance/unconfigured_banner" %>

  <%# The single most important thing to establish up front: this page and
      /privacy answer two different questions, and a reader who conflates them
      will not find what they came for. -%>
  <div class="card mb-9">
    <div class="card-head"><h2 class="text-sm font-semibold">Which document you want</h2></div>
    <div>
      <div class="px-5 py-4 border-b border-rule">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium">This page</p>
        <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-2 mt-1.5">
          Data about <strong class="text-ink">account holders</strong>: your email address, your
          team, your billing. We are the controller of it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="px-5 py-4">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium">
          <%= link_to "What Tastatur collects", privacy_path, class: "underline hover:text-signal" %>
        </p>
        <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-2 mt-1.5">
          Data collected from <strong class="text-ink">visitors to measured websites</strong>. The
          site owner is the controller of that; we are their processor. That is the page to link
          from your own privacy policy, and the page to send a visitor who asks.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-9 text-[15px] leading-relaxed">
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3">1. Who we are</h2>
      <p class="mb-3">
        <%= entity %><% if Tastatur.legal[:address].present? %>, <%= Tastatur.legal[:address] %><% end %>
        operates this Tastatur instance and is the controller of the account data described below.
      </p>
      <p>
        Contact for anything in this policy: <strong class="text-ink"><%= contact %></strong><% if Tastatur.legal[:dpo_email].present? %>.
        Data protection officer: <strong class="text-ink"><%= Tastatur.legal[:dpo_email] %></strong><% end %>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3">2. What we hold about you</h2>
      <div class="card">
        <% [
             ["Email address", "Required. It is your login and how we send confirmations, password resets and service notices.", "Contract"],
             ["Name", "Optional. Used to address you in the interface and in email.", "Contract"],
             ["Password", "Stored only as a bcrypt hash. We cannot read it and cannot tell you what it is.", "Contract"],
             ["Sign-in metadata", "Sign-in counts and timestamps, and a lock after repeated failures. Used only to secure the account.", "Legitimate interest"],
             ["Sign-in IP address", "The IP of your two most recent sign-ins to this dashboard, so you can recognise a login that was not you. This is the one place we store an IP address against an identity, it applies to you as an account holder rather than to visitors of any measured site, and it is overwritten as you sign in again.", "Legitimate interest"],
             ["Team membership", "Which accounts you belong to and your role in each.", "Contract"],
             ["Billing records", "On the hosted service only, and held by our payment processor rather than by us. We store a customer reference, never card details.", "Contract"],
             ["Server logs", "Standard web logs, which include IP addresses, retained briefly for security and debugging.", "Legitimate interest"],
             # Listed even though most readers of this page never joined it: a
             # table headed "what we hold about you" that omits a table holding
             # email addresses is the kind of omission that makes every other row
             # in it less believable. Consent rather than contract, because
             # joining is necessary neither for an account nor for anything we
             # owe you.
             ["Waitlist address", "Only if you asked to hear about a feature before it ships. We keep the address, the date you confirmed it and which page you joined from — nothing else, and no record of whether you open what we send. You confirm the address before it counts, an address never confirmed is deleted within a week, and leaving deletes it rather than moving it to a suppression list.", "Consent"]
           ].each do |what, why, basis| %>
          <div class="px-5 py-4 border-b border-rule last:border-b-0">
            <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
              <p class="label"><%= what %></p>
              <span class="text-[10px] text-muted shrink-0"><%= basis %></span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-2 mt-1.5"><%= why %></p>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </div>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-2 mt-4">
        That is the complete list. There is no profiling, no advertising, no tracking of you across
        other websites, and no automated decision-making with legal effect.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3">3. Cookies on this site</h2>
      <p class="mb-3">
        Tastatur's own dashboard sets a <strong class="text-ink">first-party session cookie</strong>
        when you sign in, and a second one if you ask to be remembered. If you switch on two-factor
        authentication and choose not to be asked for a code on a particular browser, that browser
        also gets a third — a random value, valid for thirty days, which matches a row you can
        delete from your account page at any time. All three are strictly necessary to keep you
        signed in and are exempt from consent requirements on that basis.
      </p>
      <div class="notice">
        This is not in tension with anything the product claims. The promise is that
        <em>your visitors' devices</em> get nothing. Our own application, which you have signed
        into, is a different matter, and pretending a login needs no session cookie would be
        nonsense rather than privacy.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3">4. How long we keep it</h2>
      <div class="card card-body space-y-2.5 text-sm">
        <p><strong class="text-ink">Account data:</strong> for as long as your account exists.
          Delete the account and it goes, along with the sites and measurement data belonging to it.</p>
        <p><strong class="text-ink">Server logs:</strong> a short rolling window, measured in days.</p>
        <p><strong class="text-ink">Billing records:</strong> as long as tax and accounting law
          requires, which is typically several years and is not something we can shorten on request.</p>
        <p><strong class="text-ink">Measurement data:</strong> governed by the retention period you
          set on your account, enforced nightly. Default 12 months, and 25 months is the
          maximum we offer, which is the stated ceiling for audience-measurement data.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3">5. Who else sees it</h2>
      <p class="mb-3">
        We do not sell your data, we do not share it for anyone else's marketing, and we do not
        use it to train models.
      </p>
      <%# THIS IS THE SUB-PROCESSOR LIST THE DPA PROMISES, and it now says so.
          §6 of the DPA committed us to publishing a current list and to notice
          before it changes; this table was that list, but it was not labelled as
          one, not linked from the DPA, not dated, and left the hosting provider
          unnamed — so a controller had no way to exercise the opportunity to
          object that the same sentence grants them (Art. 28(3)(d)). The anchor
          is what the DPA links to; keep it. -%>
      <p class="mb-3" id="subprocessors">
        On the hosted service, these third parties process data on our behalf. This is the
        sub-processor list referred to in
        <%= link_to "section 6 of the data processing agreement", "#{dpa_path}#subprocessors",
                    class: "underline hover:text-signal" %>.
      </p>
      <div class="card">
        <% [[Tastatur.hosting_provider_label, Tastatur.hosting_provider_description],
            ["Resend", "Delivers transactional email: confirmations, password resets, service notices."],
            ["Stripe", "Processes payments. Card details go to Stripe and never reach us."],
            # send_default_pii is false in config/initializers/sentry.rb, so an
            # IP is not attached to an error report. This said one "can
            # incidentally" be included, which over-disclosed relative to the
            # code — the right direction to be wrong, but still wrong.
            ["Sentry", "Receives error reports. IP addresses are not attached to them (Sentry's PII " \
                       "collection is switched off), though a report can include the path of the " \
                       "request that failed. Optional, and disabled if unconfigured."]].each do |who, what| %>
          <div class="px-5 py-3 border-b border-rule last:border-b-0">
            <p class="text-sm font-medium"><%= who %></p>
            <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-2 mt-1"><%= what %></p>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </div>
      <% if Tastatur.legal[:subprocessors_updated_on] %>
        <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-2 mt-3">
          This list last changed on
          <%= Tastatur.legal[:subprocessors_updated_on].to_fs(:long) %>. We give the account owner
          at least 30 days' notice by email before adding or replacing anyone on it — see
          <%= link_to "section 6 of the DPA", "#{dpa_path}#subprocessors",
                      class: "underline hover:text-signal" %>.
        </p>
      <% end %>
      <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-2 mt-4">
        <strong class="text-ink">On a self-hosted instance there are none of these</strong> unless
        the operator configures them. The operator is the only party involved.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3">6. Your rights</h2>
      <p class="mb-3">
        Where the GDPR or UK GDPR applies you may request access, correction, erasure,
        restriction, portability, or object to processing based on legitimate interest. Where the
        CCPA applies you may request access and deletion, and may not be discriminated against for
        doing so.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-3">
        Most of these you can exercise yourself, immediately, without asking us: your email and
        password are editable on the
        <%= link_to "account page", account_path, class: "underline hover:text-signal" %>, and
        deleting a site erases its measurement data irreversibly.
      </p>
      <p>
        For anything else, write to <strong class="text-ink"><%= contact %></strong>. You also have
        the right to complain to your data protection authority.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3">7. International transfers</h2>
      <p>
        Self-hosted: your data stays wherever you run it, so there is no transfer. Hosted: the
        region is stated at signup, and any transfer outside it relies on the Standard Contractual
        Clauses.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3">8. Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If we change this policy in a way that materially affects you, we will email account
        holders rather than quietly editing the page and relying on you to notice.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="rule pt-6 text-sm text-muted">
      <p>
<%# Never Date.current: that made every legal document claim it had been revised
          today, on every single page load. A reader checking whether the terms changed
          since they agreed to them was told "today" regardless. Unset renders nothing,
          which is honest, rather than a date we invented. %>
      <% if Tastatur.legal[:updated_on] %>Last updated <%= Tastatur.legal[:updated_on].to_fs(:long) %>.<% end %>
        See also the <%= link_to "terms", terms_path, class: "underline" %>,
        the <%= link_to "processor terms", dpa_path, class: "underline" %>, and
        <%= link_to "what the product collects from visitors", privacy_path, class: "underline" %>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
